<h2>The design of the provision</h2>
<p>Section 26 has three limbs. Sub-section (1) makes the reliefs of Sections 18 to 22 available in any legal proceeding before a civil court, family court or criminal court affecting the aggrieved person and the respondent. Sub-section (2) clarifies that such relief may be sought in addition to, and along with, any other relief claimed in that proceeding. Sub-section (3) imposes the coordination duty: if any relief is obtained in such other proceedings, the aggrieved person is bound to inform the Magistrate before whom her DV application, if any, is pending. The structure reflects the Act&rsquo;s character as a civil-remedial statute wearing a criminal-procedure coat — its reliefs are protective and compensatory, and Parliament saw no reason to confine them to one doorway.</p>

<h2>Where Section 26 earns its keep</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>In a pending divorce.</strong> A wife respondent to (or petitioner in) a divorce can seek protection and residence orders from the Family Court itself, avoiding a parallel Magistrate&rsquo;s file with its own dates and its own delays.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>In a custody case.</strong> Temporary custody under Section 21 can be sought in guardianship proceedings, keeping the child&rsquo;s arrangements and the domestic-violence context before one judge.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>In a maintenance petition.</strong> Monetary relief under Section 20 — which extends beyond maintenance to loss of earnings, medical expenses and loss caused by destruction or removal of property — can supplement a claim under Section 144 of the BNSS.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>In a civil suit.</strong> Where property or possession litigation is already pending between the parties, residence protection can be sought in that suit rather than through a fresh forum.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategic considerations</h2>
<p>The choice between a standalone Section 12 application and a Section 26 claim is practical, not doctrinal. The Magistrate&rsquo;s forum offers the Act&rsquo;s full procedural kit — Protection Officers, domestic incident reports, the Section 23 power to grant ex parte interim orders, and the Section 31 offence for breach of protection orders. The Section 26 route offers consolidation: one court, one record, one set of interim orders governing parties who are already before it. Three cautions matter. First, the relief claimed must still satisfy the DV Act&rsquo;s own thresholds — a domestic relationship, an aggrieved person, and allegations amounting to domestic violence as defined in Section 3. Second, the duty of disclosure under Section 26(3) is not a formality; concealment of parallel orders invites adverse inference and recall. Third, monetary reliefs across forums are set off against each other — the law compensates once, whichever door the claim entered through.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Relief</th><th>DV Act provision</th><th>Available via Section 26 in</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Protection order</td><td>Section 18</td><td>Civil, family or criminal proceedings between the parties</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Residence order</td><td>Section 19</td><td>Divorce, partition, possession and allied proceedings</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Monetary relief</td><td>Section 20</td><td>Maintenance and matrimonial proceedings</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Temporary custody</td><td>Section 21</td><td>Guardianship and matrimonial proceedings</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Compensation</td><td>Section 22</td><td>Any qualifying proceeding, subject to set-off discipline</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Drafting the Section 26 application</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>Plead the DV foundations.</strong> The host proceeding supplies the forum, not the cause: the application must independently establish the domestic relationship and particularised acts of domestic violence.</p>
  <p><strong>Specify the relief by section.</strong> Identify whether Section 18, 19, 20, 21 or 22 relief is sought, with the precise orders proposed — courts act on drafted orders, not grievances.</p>
  <p><strong>Disclose parallel proceedings.</strong> List every pending case between the parties and every order obtained, and undertake the Section 26(3) intimation where a DV application exists.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This explainer is general information on Section 26 of the DV Act, not advice on any particular matter. The appropriate forum and relief depend on the facts and the state of existing proceedings.</div>
